1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,200
Question eight.

2
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:07,917
Step one: macro scan. It asks the access method that jumps straight to a time point.

3
00:00:07,917 --> 00:00:10,956
One mark. Command words: “closest to”.

4
00:00:10,956 --> 00:00:12,395
Step two: denoise.

5
00:00:12,395 --> 00:00:16,794
Useful, in yellow: chosen time slot, video frame, disk.

6
00:00:16,794 --> 00:00:22,791
In cyan: “can jump to” and “no need to read frame by frame from the start”.

7
00:00:22,791 --> 00:00:27,189
Useless red herring: the traffic content — kick it out.

8
00:00:27,189 --> 00:00:28,629
Step three: draft.

9
00:00:28,629 --> 00:00:31,908
Can jump means no reading from the start.

10
00:00:31,908 --> 00:00:35,346
No reading from the start is direct access.

11
00:00:35,346 --> 00:00:37,585
Disks support direct access.

12
00:00:37,585 --> 00:00:38,945
Step four: P O E.

13
00:00:38,945 --> 00:00:44,383
A, sequential access: must read from the start — the opposite — cut.

14
00:00:44,383 --> 00:00:48,461
B, tape-only serial read: the stem says disk — cut.

15
00:00:48,461 --> 00:00:53,419
C, download whole file first: no such limit in the stem — cut.

16
00:00:53,419 --> 00:00:59,977
Step five: sanity check and lock. Jumping to a chosen frame matches direct access.

17
00:00:59,977 --> 00:01:02,136
So the correct answer is D.

